**Serious finding remains: yes — cross-arm question identity is not yet defined for the acceptance comparison.****A serious finding remains:** the plan says every new regression stops an arm, but also says a newly opened trivial question will only be counted. For example, if the control keeps a minor candidate as evidence and the changed matcher opens it as a separate question, the plan gives two different acceptance rules.

1. **Serious — a new trivial question can pass.** [Plan lines 155–156]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:155) make every candidate that was correct in the control and wrong in the new arm a stopping condition. But [line 165]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:165) exempts a distinct but unworthy question from stopping a matching arm. The [signed standard]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:281) requires both distinctness and worth. The plan needs to distinguish an **existing** unworthy opening, which can be reported as a remaining defect, from a **new** one caused by the changed arm, which its own regression rule says must stop it.

2. **Factual — the placement diagram overstates the one-home rule.** [Plan lines 33–34]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:33) say placement files each statement under the narrowest existing question **or** this article’s candidate. In [placement.py]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/placement.py:63), candidate links from extraction are retained and the placer’s existing-question links are added. A statement can therefore have both. This matters when recording every expected evidence destination.

3. **Factual — 94 statements without a question are not 94 questions never proposed.** [Plan line 124]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:124) uses D212’s count to describe an extraction coverage gap. [D212]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/DECISIONS.md:2705) says 94 **material records** had no corresponding question during the week; it does not establish that no candidate was proposed. The [underlying trace]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-28-retention-audit/root-trace-2026-10-28.md:7) also cautions that these are records, not 94 different facts. Losses common to both arms remain outside this review’s measurement scope.

The per-candidate rule otherwise addresses the round-4 examples: it represents required successors, distinguishes worth from identity, checks successor evidence, and adds an expiry test. The round-9 fix also provides a way to translate question IDs across arms. The stored v38 same-article 2026 and 2027 spending questions remain a known hard case: [the plan records their missing succession relationship but does not propose changing it]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:119); the [PRD]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/06-views.md:133) says successive periods form a chain.

I verified the main census totals by rerunning the read-only script, and verified the 20 shared evidence records in v38. The guarantee-and-financing example is in both the [signed standard]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:314) and the [collision prompt]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/prompts/collision-v1.txt:1); the plan correctly leaves its causal role in the ACP split unproven. The verdict-category census still parses reason text, as the plan says. I could not independently verify every archived verdict, cost estimate, or the full question context behind the “A” replies in section 11b.

**Serious finding remains: yes.****Serious findings remain.** The plan’s revised checks can still miss a lost question or a wrong merge, and one central example compares different requests as if they were the same pair.

1. **The gold still has a cross-object blind spot.** [Plan lines 104–105]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:104) add unlisted pairs only for the same object and group G2 by object. In the sealed ledger, `v40` is an Ohio power-allocation question under `chip-export-controls`; `v44` asks about the Pike County arrangement under `NVDA`. The earlier review labels `v44` a duplicate of `v40` ([gold, lines 46–50](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-admission-v12-2026-10-26/docs/research/2026-10-28-quality-review/gold-admission.md:46)). At `v44`’s creation, `v40` was absent from its recorded candidate list in the [sealed ledger](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/sealed-2026-10-28-v34-nvda-3m-through-0828/views.sqlite). Whether they are truly the same question needs a source-based decision; the proposed gold would not require that comparison.

2. **The regression checks can still pass a wrong destination.** [Plan lines 129–131](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:129) define a new false merge only where the control did **not** merge. If the control merges a candidate correctly into A and the new arm merges it incorrectly into B, that definition misses it. Requiring its evidence to land under B then passes the wrong destination by construction. D207 requires a statement’s appropriate, narrowest home, not merely the destination selected by matching ([D207](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/DECISIONS.md:2630)). The independent reading of each merge helps, but the stated acceptance rule needs to compare the chosen destination with the gold.

3. **G2′ catches only the required questions already named in two older sets.** [Plan line 106](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:106) does not require a complete source-derived list for the five ledgers. A required question outside those cases can disappear without failing “必开丢失.” D188 describes a 20-case gold, not a complete reference for this corpus ([D188](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/DECISIONS.md:2483)). Fixed extraction also cannot recover a required question that extraction never proposed; that limitation needs to be explicit in the score.

4. **The ACP inconsistency is misidentified.** [Plan lines 16–18 and 76](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:16) say the judge gave opposite answers for the same `v1`/`v3` pair. In the [v38 ledger](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/english-v38-nvda-1w-2026-10-28/views.sqlite), match record 6 compared the July 2 candidate that became `v3` with `v1` and said distinct. Record 20 compared a **new July 6 candidate** with both `v1` and `v3` and said it was the same as each. These are different candidate texts and contexts, so they do not prove the same request is inconsistent. The archived answers do give reasons, correcting round 1’s statement, but do not establish which instruction caused them. The quoted guarantee/financing sentence **is** in both the [prompt](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/prompts/collision-v1.txt:1) and [signed standard](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:318). The boundary may need clarification; the stored example does not by itself prove that sentence conflicts with D210 or caused the split.

5. **H1’s proposed shortcut is broader than its cited rule.** [Plan line 90](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:90) leans toward merging because the same annual-report note checks both amounts. A single document can report two independently moving amounts. The standard requires the *same result* to settle both and also requires scope to distinguish financing from guarantees ([standard, lines 293–294 and 316–319](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:293)). D210’s one-deal-or-programme wording does not establish that sharing a note makes two questions identical ([D210](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/DECISIONS.md:2670)).

6. **H2 describes a later question without yet showing how it survives until then.** [Plan lines 91 and 171](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:91) merge a later deadline into an earlier question, then propose opening a successor after expiry. The earlier question keeps its fixed deadline and outcomes ([PRD 06, lines 74–75](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/06-views.md:74)); the present succession path acts when matching a candidate ([code](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/views/jev_matching.py:284)). The plan leaves the later deadline’s preservation and the event that creates the successor to a future table. Without those, Kyber’s 2028 question can be lost if no new article arrives after 2027.

7. **The holdout repair is incomplete.** [Plan lines 111–113](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:111) give the holdout only one blind labeller, so there is no second independent label with which to identify the “disagreements” it says Alex will decide. The working rule requires a blind second reader and recorded resolution ([AGENTS, lines 246–251](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/AGENTS.md:246)). Also, the plan does not say how holdout results stay out of subsequent tuning when each arm is measured. The separation of the author from the labels is an improvement, but it does not yet establish that control.

8. **The first-50 summary repeats a source’s arithmetic error.** [Plan lines 8–9 and 80](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:8) report four borderline questions. Counting the actual rows in [the earlier gold](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-admission-v12-2026-10-26/docs/research/2026-10-28-quality-review/gold-admission.md:15) gives **23 keep, 12 duplicate, 10 do not open, and 5 borderline**; its summary line says 24 and 4. The 22 duplicate-or-do-not-open labels are correct as that reader’s judgments, but they remain unapproved judgments rather than established defects.

I re-ran the corrected census read-only: **122 candidates, 39 merges and 2 successions** for the sealed ledger, and **32 candidates, 12 merges and 1 succession** for v38. The reported evidence counts match the census outputs, subject to the plan’s correct qualification that “same claim” labels are estimates. H3 now names the conflicting milestone rule; H4 is presented as a choice; and H7’s lean preserves D204. I did not independently verify every cost estimate, every archived verdict classification, or every factual claim in the plan.

**Serious findings remain**, chiefly the gold’s coverage and the acceptance checks’ ability to pass a wrong destination. No files were edited.
