    11	4. **The claimed cause of the ACP split is unproven.** [Plan lines 12–16 and 68](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:12) attribute the split to the “guarantee beside a financing” sentence. The sentence **is** in both [the collision prompt](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/prompts/collision-v1.txt:1) and [the signed standard](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:318); the plan is correct on that factual point. But v38 match records 6 and 10 record only the verdicts, not the instruction that caused them. The plan itself acknowledges that the model account has not been obtained ([lines 75–76](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:75)). The two standard lines create a real boundary to resolve, but the records do not establish that this sentence caused the split or that every guarantee and financing within a plan must be separate.
    12	
    13	5. **H1, H3 and the general rule add boundaries that the standard does not settle.** [Plan lines 80–82 and 86–88](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:80) add “when uncertain, merge,” “one plan, one question,” and “one product generation, one question.” The signed standard does say one outcome can settle two questions ([line 316](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:316)), so that part is correctly attributed. It also requires separation where answers can move apart ([line 274](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:274)), and [D236](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/DECISIONS.md:2871) permits a launch **or** shipment question; it does not decide that all milestones are one question. D243’s caution about noisy extraction is not a decision to merge uncertain questions ([standard lines 153–156](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:153)). The v38 ledger’s `v11` launch and `v17` shipment are a concrete case requiring that distinction.
    14	
    15	6. **H4 treats different periods as a settled duplicate.** [Plan line 89](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:89) says FY2027 CPU revenue and calendar-2026 server CPU revenue are the same question under the standard. They differ in both scope and period. The standard’s duplicate test concerns another presentation of the **same result** ([line 289](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:289)); it separately treats the next period as succession ([line 317](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:317)). The earlier [Claude-labelled review](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-admission-v12-2026-10-26/docs/research/2026-10-28-quality-review/gold-admission.md:30) labels this pair duplicate, but is not an Alex-approved decision.
    16	
    17	7. **H2 and H5 do not fully cover the existing identity contract.** H2’s proposed merge keeps the earlier question’s fixed outcomes and due date; the plan does not show how a later deadline remains answerable after the earlier deadline passes. The existing question and outcomes are immutable ([PRD 06 lines 8 and 14](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/06-views.md:8)); succession is continued tracking after an event question closes ([line 176](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/06-views.md:176)). H5 says code would retain only object, type and due year ([plan line 90](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:90)); that omits scope, event type and other distinctions currently used by [identity.py](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/views/identity.py:18). It could coalesce separate events, and the plan does not enumerate that risk.
    18	
   107	- **No case-fitted fix.** A hand list, a special path or a rule fitted to the case in view fixes that case and breaks the
   108	  next. First ask whether the judge had the material and the definition it needed, and whether a typed field is missing;
   109	  run the proposal against the existing tests and the gold for a counter-example before building (D167: "我不要
   110	  hardcode 解法 it's premature optimization, not general root fix").
   111	- A blind review measures the tables. A serious finding they do not hold shows a missing dimension: add it and walk
   112	  every row again; never answer the finding alone. Code starts when the review finds nothing serious.
   113	- A design whose core contract is still open is a question, not something to build.
   114	- A foundation's acceptance carries a size and speed budget from its first control, not only correctness.
   115	
   116	## 3. Writing code
   117	
   118	- **One owner.** A rule lives where the data is written, and refuses: a warning that lets the run continue is a silent
   119	  failure. Fix the writer and delete its duplicate implementations in the same change; when a fix lands in one
   120	  consumer, search every consumer of the same key or rule.
   121	- **Code over prose.** A contract that code or a schema can enforce (an enum over allowed keys, a shape) is enforced
   122	  there, and stays out of prompts and documents.
   123	- **One word per concept.** The glossary in `docs/prd/00-system.md` (section Glossary; D36, D163) names each concept
   124	  once, and `docs/check.py` fails on a word it rules out. A new concept gets its row and its ruled-out words in the same
   125	  change; a new ruled-out word must catch the sentence it was written against and pass the corrected one.
   126	- **Identity.** An identifier names one thing for the life of its scope; a changed thing gets a new id. Equal values
   127	  alone never establish identity.
   128	- **Explicit inputs.** Library code takes runtime paths and configuration as arguments. A branch a run takes comes from
   129	  its configuration, never from whether an environment variable happens to be set; a configured step whose credential
   130	  is missing refuses the run. A component that sends a model call uses the transport its caller passes.
   131	- **Never order by the wall clock.** It steps both ways; order comes from a sequence the program owns: dispatch's
   132	  sequence number. The stream never reads the calendar; only pull mode acts on time, with its "now" passed in (D12,
   133	  D13, D31).
   134	- **Strictness is code too.** A check added to prevent defects can itself fail; accept it on real data, not samples.
